  "scope": "Immutable C++/Python SelectionRegion retains complete local area geometry and an explicit XY origin, independently of a document canvas. Region enlargement retains off-image irregular cells, holes and disconnected islands across repeated edits. Explicit projection returns a legacy canvas selection; vector projection translates without clamping. SelectionEditor.enlarged_region exposes the full path. Source shape/context, origins, bounds, style, resource admission, cancellation and finish failures are qualified within the stated limits. The complete ImageJ oracle exposed a one-cell saturated-byte placement rule and native rectangle promotion: both now match in the full-region API, so later operations take the correct analytic path. A shared private kernel policy preserves legacy clipped command geometry and record equations. Geometry/projection estimates now include at least their own admission floor, exact working caps are exercised, projection admission is cancellable, and the Python estimate wrapper copies mutable limits before GIL release. C++ callbacks can assign over their source value; enlargement, projection and bounds now freeze complete source geometry/canvas/origin before callbacks, with an observed failing preimage and native/installed SDK regression checks. This is a required foundation, not editor-level gap completion: document storage, saved rows, codecs, commands, interchange, workflow/batch retention and actual UI interaction still require canonical region migration. No new physical UI acceptance is claimed; existing UI regressions are rerun. Legacy canvas callers also retain previously accepted far-off-canvas double coordinates, independently of the new integer region-frame limit. They clip directly without a temporary full-region packed result. Actual historical V1 command data verifies validation/rebind/preparation compatibility on both hosts, in addition to native/installed SDK checks.",
